Raisse the Thaumaturge wrote:
 > Gabriel Linder wrote:

 >> I am glad that nethack is written in C, because
 >> not every system has a java/.net/whatever
 >> compiler/framework available but every system has
 >> a C compiler.

Pretty much no two of them are alike in how they
interface with their hardware and OS environment,
too, so software portability in C is a really,
really ugly "conditional compilation" issue.

Java tries to address that, by being "as portable as
possible" among hardware/OS mixes, but still fails
by a little bit.

 >> I found some roguelikes written using Pascal or
 >> .Net and cannot try them because my system was
 >> lacking such things. And even now it has them, I
 >> would not install something like Mono or Java
 >> just to play a roguelike.

Java is indeed a bit of a slug if you install the
entire compilation environment, which with needed
documentation is many hundreds of megabytes, but
if you just want to run some software built by
others, the Java runtime kit isn't so terrible.

 > You'd only need the compiler to compile your own
 > game. If it's distributed as a binary, why would
 > you care what language it had been written in?

Java executables aren't distributed as binaries,
they're distributed as *.jar archive files of Java
byte-code files (the output of the Java compiler), so
a Java version would indeed require the Java runtime
system for executing it [but it is pretty nearly a
hopeless proposition to use the WWW without that
Java runtime anyway, too many web pages depend on
it, so the _added_ overhead for a typical WEBizen
would probably be zero].

The larger question about a Java port of NetHack is,
with Sun MicroSystems teetering on the edge of
financial collapse: is vendor-captive programming
language Java still a viable long term programming
language?  The freeware GNU version of Java is
nothing like the "official" version of Java, so
porting skills or code to it would be a mess.

 > (playing devil's advocate here; I do compile my
 > own)

I wish I could. The WinOS compile, which should be
utterly transparent, done using the existing how to
compile NetHack cookbook, fails for me with opaque
error messages that have no particular obvious
relationship to the place where the error occurs.

